WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.767
<v The Announcer>Previously, on LAiDIES: our heroine
stopped feeling behind, and finally

00:00:05.767 --> 00:00:11.453
<v The Announcer>stopped putting it off — one avoided
email, nine seconds flat, and the

00:00:11.453 --> 00:00:17.380
<v The Announcer>realization that AI is just the most
talented new hire she'll ever manage.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:24.051
<v The Announcer>And on this episode: she types something
vague into the machine, gets back a

00:00:24.051 --> 00:00:31.420
<v The Announcer>paragraph of absolutely nothing... and
learns that talking to AI isn't coding —
it's delegation.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.300
<v The Announcer>This is Episode Two: Tell Me What You
Want.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.700
<v The Heroine>It's nine-fifteen on a Tuesday, and I am
losing a staring contest with a
paragraph.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.857
<v The Heroine>I'd asked one of the AI tools to draft
talking points for a meeting, and what

00:00:47.857 --> 00:00:50.180
<v The Heroine>it gave back is... technically words.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.726
<v The Heroine>"Leverage synergies." "Drive alignment."
"Circle back to maximize stakeholder

00:00:56.726 --> 00:01:03.000
<v The Heroine>buy-in." It reads like a motivational
poster that went to business school and
came back worse.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:11.300
<v The Heroine>And the maddening part? Yesterday, the
same tool wrote me a project update for
my director that I barely had to touch.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.100
<v The Heroine>Same app. Same me.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.520
<v The Heroine>Same tragic office lighting.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.080
<v The Heroine>One day it reads my mind, and the next
it hands me a throw pillow.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:27.724
<v The Heroine>And I couldn't help but wonder... why
does AI read my mind some days, and

00:01:27.724 --> 00:01:29.980
<v The Heroine>completely ignore me on others?

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.621
<v The Heroine>Welcome back to LAiDIES — smart, busy
women learning AI one Wednesday at a

00:01:35.621 --> 00:01:38.590
<v The Heroine>time, from a little internet town called
SUNNYVAiLE.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.995
<v The Heroine>Last week, for your try-on, you held
auditions: one task, all three tools,

00:01:44.995 --> 00:01:47.890
<v The Heroine>just to see how differently they each
answer.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.630
<v The Heroine>So you already know the tool has
opinions.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.890
<v The Heroine>This week is your half of the
conversation — the ask itself.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:01.990
<v The Heroine>Because it turns out that matters even
more than which tool you pick.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:09.090
<v The Heroine>So I did what I do with any problem I
can't out-stubborn at my desk: I took it
to town.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:14.950
<v The Heroine>Corner table at the Blend and Snap, oat
latte, K-S-V-L on low.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:20.245
<v The Heroine>And I pulled them both back up — the
word-salad talking points from that

00:02:20.245 --> 00:02:26.430
<v The Heroine>morning, and the director update I'd
barely had to touch the day before — and
set them side by side.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.390
<v The Heroine>It wasn't the tool that changed between
the two.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.490
<v The Heroine>It was me: what I'd typed to get each
one.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:40.379
<v The Heroine>Because here's the punchline, and it
took me an embarrassingly long time to

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:42.530
<v The Heroine>see it: AI can't read your mind.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:47.670
<v The Heroine>What's in your head stays in your
head... until you type it out.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:51.150
<v The Heroine>And that word everyone throws around —
prompt?

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.650
<v The Heroine>The computer nerds didn't even invent
it.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.850
<v The Heroine>They lifted it from the theater kids.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:03.030
<v The Heroine>The two lunch tables that never once
overlapped — and the AV club quietly

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:08.450
<v The Heroine>swiped 'prompt' right off the drama
club. Think about your coffee order.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:14.301
<v The Heroine>At your regular spot, you barely have to
say it — they know your usual, because

00:03:14.301 --> 00:03:16.890
<v The Heroine>you built that over a hundred Tuesdays.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.930
<v The Heroine>But AI is not your regular spot.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:26.683
<v The Heroine>It's the brand-new café across town, and
if you breeze in and say "the usual,"

00:03:26.683 --> 00:03:31.505
<v The Heroine>you get a blank look — or a plain drip
going cold on the counter, which you

00:03:31.505 --> 00:03:37.470
<v The Heroine>can't even be mad about, because you
never told the barista what you actually
wanted to drink.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:44.030
<v The Heroine>And the AI isn't holding out on you — it
genuinely can't see what you didn't say.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:47.030
<v The Heroine>It has your words, and nothing else.

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:53.190
<v The Heroine>Not your job, not who's going to read
this, not the meeting sitting on your
calendar.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:58.770
<v The Heroine>Every detail you leave in your head is
one it's forced to guess at.

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:05.810
<v The Heroine>So you spell it out — every time — and
the guessing stops. That's prompting.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:11.730
<v The Heroine>So how long until it knows your order by
heart, like your regular spot does?

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.510
<v The Heroine>Right now — it doesn't.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:19.070
<v The Heroine>Every new chat, you walk into the café a
stranger again.

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:25.763
<v The Heroine>But you can teach it to remember you —
your usual, your voice, the way you like

00:04:25.763 --> 00:04:28.730
<v The Heroine>things — so you stop starting from zero.

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:32.570
<v The Heroine>That's an episode of its own, and we'll
get there.

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:36.470
<v The Heroine>For today: assume it forgot, and tell it
again.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:43.510
<v The Heroine>The Spice Girls had this figured out
decades ago. (Wait... Decades? Ooff.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:49.721
<v The Heroine>That is a jagged little pill to swallow,
isn't it...) "Tell me what you want,

00:04:49.721 --> 00:04:55.339
<v The Heroine>what you really, really want." Those
iconic lyrics from the Spice Girls were

00:04:55.339 --> 00:04:59.150
<v The Heroine>not gesturing at a vibe. They wanted
specifics.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.250
<v The Heroine>AI is exactly the same.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:08.401
<v The Heroine>It will zig-a-zig-ah all day long — but
it only does useful work when you tell

00:05:08.401 --> 00:05:10.250
<v The Heroine>it precisely what you need.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:17.030
<v The Heroine>Which is why the patron saint of this
episode is David Rose. I know, I know.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:23.070
<v The Heroine>You're thinking, "But he's not from the
nineties, or the two-thousands?" And
you're right.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.530
<v The Heroine>But he is so fabulous that we just had
to make one exception.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:32.610
<v The Heroine>In the immortal words of Shakespeare: A
Rose by any other name...

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:39.263
<v The Heroine>If you've seen Schitt's Creek, you know
David is pathologically specific — the

00:05:39.263 --> 00:05:42.790
<v The Heroine>wine, the sweater, the exact drape of
the sweater.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:49.202
<v The Heroine>And you remember Moira telling him to
"fold in the cheese," and him standing

00:05:49.202 --> 00:05:54.673
<v The Heroine>in that kitchen screaming, "WHAT DOES
THAT MEAN?!" That is your AI, every

00:05:54.673 --> 00:06:00.070
<v The Heroine>single time you type "write me an email
about the project." It is trying.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:06.613
<v The Heroine>But you handed it "fold in the cheese" —
when what it needed was "scrape the

00:06:06.613 --> 00:06:12.395
<v The Heroine>spatula along the bottom of the bowl,
lift, turn it over, turn the bowl, and

00:06:12.395 --> 00:06:18.328
<v The Heroine>repeat." And yes, full confession: I had
AI write that cooking description for

00:06:18.328 --> 00:06:23.735
<v The Heroine>me, because I do not cook. (Oh, and
P-S-A: if you haven't seen Schitt's

00:06:23.735 --> 00:06:30.630
<v The Heroine>Creek, what are you even doing with your
life? Get on it!) Ok, back to what I
did.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:35.130
<v The Heroine>The fix, it turned out, was something I
already knew how to do.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:40.937
<v The Heroine>I stopped typing at AI like a Google
search — three vague words, fingers

00:06:40.937 --> 00:06:46.150
<v The Heroine>crossed — and I started briefing it like
a smart new hire in her first week.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:52.410
<v The Heroine>Who is this for? What do they care
about? What's the tone? How long?

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.750
<v The Heroine>What should it absolutely not include?

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.270
<v The Heroine>Have we done one of these before that
she can copy from?

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:05.270
<v The Heroine>Every one of those is a question you
already ask when you hand work to a
person.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:09.670
<v The Heroine>Prompting isn't technical. It's
delegation.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:16.283
<v The Heroine>I even put those on a card for your
study pack — a handful of questions,

00:07:16.283 --> 00:07:21.690
<v The Heroine>thirty seconds, and your ask stops being
a shrug and starts being an instruction.

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:28.370
<v The Heroine>But if you only ever steal one, steal
the last: show it an example.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:35.497
<v The Heroine>Paste in one you already love — an email
that landed, a summary that worked — and

00:07:35.497 --> 00:07:42.170
<v The Heroine>say "match this." Nothing gets you a
better answer faster than handing it
something to copy.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:47.450
<v The Heroine>Let me show you, because the difference
is almost rude.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:51.830
<v The Heroine>Say you've got a twelve-page policy
change to get in front of a meeting.

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:58.781
<v The Heroine>The lazy ask — what you'd type without
LAiDIES — is just: "summarize this

00:07:58.781 --> 00:08:03.850
<v The Heroine>policy change for my stakeholders." And
back comes... everything.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:10.290
<v The Heroine>A wall of text — accurate, thorough, and
completely useless in the two minutes
you've got.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:16.511
<v The Heroine>Background, rationale, timelines,
exemptions, appendices A through D — all

00:08:16.511 --> 00:08:22.150
<v The Heroine>weighted exactly the same, because it
can't tell what matters to you from what
doesn't.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:27.669
<v The Heroine>It's Ross, screaming "PIVOT" —
technically a direction, zero help

00:08:27.669 --> 00:08:29.870
<v The Heroine>getting the couch up the stairs.

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.130
<v The Heroine>You still have to do the reading.

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:39.590
<v The Heroine>So you try it again — only this time you
brief it out loud, the way you'd brief a
person.

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:46.510
<v The Heroine>Something like: "Summarize this for six
senior managers who have two minutes
before a meeting.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:51.595
<v The Heroine>Tell them what's changing, when it takes
effect, what their teams actually do

00:08:51.595 --> 00:08:55.930
<v The Heroine>differently, and whether it touches
budget. Cut the backstory.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:58.990
<v The Heroine>Bullets — about a hundred and fifty
words.

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:05.474
<v The Heroine>And here's last quarter's summary that
landed — match it." And this time, back

00:09:05.474 --> 00:09:11.096
<v The Heroine>comes something you would actually send:
a subject line, then a handful of tight

00:09:11.096 --> 00:09:14.150
<v The Heroine>bullets, in exactly the shape you asked
for.

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:19.844
<v The Heroine>And buried in one of them — a line you
would never have caught skimming twelve

00:09:19.844 --> 00:09:24.496
<v The Heroine>pages in two minutes: contractors are
exempt until the new year, so your

00:09:24.496 --> 00:09:26.790
<v The Heroine>vendor team is on a different clock.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:31.670
<v The Heroine>That one line is the whole reason you
opened AI.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:36.940
<v The Heroine>You could have written the summary
yourself — what you could not have done,

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:40.761
<v The Heroine>not in the twenty minutes before the
meeting, was read twelve pages of

00:09:40.761 --> 00:09:45.550
<v The Heroine>compliance language and catch the one
exemption that would have blown the
whole thing up.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:52.726
<v The Heroine>That's not a writing job; it's a reading
job — and AI just did it, tailored to

00:09:52.726 --> 00:09:54.850
<v The Heroine>exactly what you asked for.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:10:01.450
<v The Heroine>Ninety seconds to write, forty-five to
check — forward it without touching a
word.

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:07.653
<v The Heroine>A full "It's Britney, bitch" moment —
that unbothered confidence of asking for

00:10:07.653 --> 00:10:11.670
<v The Heroine>exactly what you wanted, and getting
exactly what you needed.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:15.290
<v The Heroine>And if the first answer isn't quite
right?

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:22.090
<v The Heroine>You don't start from scratch — you tell
it what's off, exactly like you would
with a real new hire.

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:29.314
<v The Heroine>It's calling into the radio and asking
for exactly the song you want to hear

00:10:29.314 --> 00:10:35.418
<v The Heroine>(or finally shelling out twenty bucks at
H-M-V for the CD), not spinning the

00:10:35.418 --> 00:10:38.430
<v The Heroine>dial, hoping it lands on one you like.

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:45.406
<v The Heroine>And if a voice in your head is going
"briefing, context, knowing what to cut

00:10:45.406 --> 00:10:50.490
<v The Heroine>— that sounds a little soft to be a real
skill" — hold that thought.

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:56.975
<v The Heroine>Because later that week I went looking,
at the LIBRAiRY in town, the reference

00:10:56.975 --> 00:11:01.730
<v The Heroine>desk where you go to actually look
things up — and I found the proof.

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:06.870
<v The Heroine>A study, Harvard and BCG, over seven
hundred consultants.

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:12.878
<v The Heroine>On the right kind of task, the ones
using AI worked about twenty-five

00:11:12.878 --> 00:11:18.510
<v The Heroine>percent faster and turned out work rated
roughly forty percent higher in quality.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:22.910
<v The Heroine>But the thing that separated the winners
was not technical.

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:29.410
<v The Heroine>It was knowing how to brief, how to hand
over context, when to push back.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:35.764
<v The Heroine>And the best line about it comes from
someone who co-authored that very study

00:11:35.764 --> 00:11:41.118
<v The Heroine>— Ethan Mollick, a Wharton professor and
one of the most-read, most-trusted

00:11:41.118 --> 00:11:45.010
<v The Heroine>voices on actually using AI. Here's what
he said:

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:50.110
<v The Expert>The skills that are so often dismissed
as soft turned out to be the hard ones.

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:55.380
<v The Heroine>Which means the woman who writes a
thorough project brief — who thinks

00:11:55.380 --> 00:12:00.316
<v The Heroine>about her reader, spells out exactly
what she needs, knows what to cut — is

00:12:00.316 --> 00:12:06.030
<v The Heroine>already ahead of the guy who types three
words and expects the machine to read
his mind.

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:12.990
<v The Heroine>And here's the part worth sitting with.
Communicating clearly. Thinking
critically.

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.190
<v The Heroine>Reading what a person actually needs.

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:23.879
<v The Heroine>Those got filed under "soft skills" for
a generation — soft, as in nice-to-have,

00:12:23.879 --> 00:12:28.070
<v The Heroine>as in not the real work. The consolation
prize.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:34.665
<v The Heroine>But study after study now lands in the
same place: in the age of AI, those are

00:12:34.665 --> 00:12:37.970
<v The Heroine>the skills that separate the people who
win.

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:42.530
<v The Heroine>And not instead of the hard skills — on
top of them.

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:49.392
<v The Heroine>So if you're a sharp communicator with
real judgment who also knows her stuff —

00:12:49.392 --> 00:12:51.730
<v The Heroine>that was never the soft skill set.

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:54.090
<v The Heroine>That's the one that wins.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:58.910
<v The Heroine>And it's exactly what makes this thing
work for you.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.670
<v The Heroine>Which brings me to my favourite part of
every episode.

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:09.000
<v The Heroine>We call it the cocktail party
explanation — the line you can actually

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:15.082
<v The Heroine>say out loud at happy hour when someone
asks, "so, what's a prompt, really?"

00:13:15.082 --> 00:13:19.030
<v The Heroine>Here it is: a prompt isn't code. It's a
delegation.

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:23.710
<v The Heroine>You're not programming a machine —
you're briefing an assistant.

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:26.570
<v The Heroine>And you already know how to brief.

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:32.590
<v The Heroine>You have been doing it your whole
career, for people who needed clearer
instructions.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:38.617
<v The Heroine>This one is just faster, never sleeps,
and will absolutely fold the cheese

00:13:38.617 --> 00:13:40.770
<v The Heroine>wrong if you don't tell her how.

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:44.490
<v The Heroine>Oh — and the first person I tried that
line on?

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:48.390
<v The Heroine>A friend who's been "meaning to get to
this" for about a year.

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:50.890
<v The Heroine>She got it in one sentence.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:57.234
<v The Heroine>So on my way out of town, I stopped by
the Post Office and mailed her a

00:13:57.234 --> 00:14:02.968
<v The Heroine>postcard — the kind you send from a
place you actually love — and wrote on

00:14:02.968 --> 00:14:08.090
<v The Heroine>the back: because SUNNYVAiLE is even
better with your people in it.

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:11.190
<v The Heroine>And... that's the episode.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:13.790
<v The Heroine>Now — your try-on.

00:14:14.690 --> 00:14:20.150
<v The Heroine>Ten minutes, not homework, and
everything you need lives at laidies.ai.

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:27.530
<v The Heroine>That's "LAiDIES" spelled with an i in
the middle: L, A, i, D, I, E, S.

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:33.297
<v The Heroine>And if you'd rather read than listen —
or you want to see that before-and-after

00:14:33.297 --> 00:14:38.510
<v The Heroine>with your own eyes — the whole episode's
written up there too, laid out side by
side.

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:44.170
<v The Heroine>This week: hand one real task to an AI
tool twice.

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:50.290
<v The Heroine>First the lazy way — three vague words,
the way you'd Google it.

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:56.607
<v The Heroine>Then the David Rose way: who it's for,
what they care about, the tone, the

00:14:56.607 --> 00:14:58.870
<v The Heroine>length, and what to leave out.

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:01.910
<v The Heroine>Put the two answers side by side.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:05.630
<v The Heroine>The difference isn't the tool getting
smarter between tries.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.310
<v The Heroine>It's you getting specific.

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:15.645
<v The Heroine>Then, if you're feeling it: grab this
week's study pack at the Blend and Snap,

00:15:15.645 --> 00:15:21.958
<v The Heroine>take the pop quiz at SUNNYVAiLE High —
ten questions, plus two bonus, banking

00:15:21.958 --> 00:15:28.272
<v The Heroine>butterfly clips in your closet — and
turn on K-S-V-L, ninety-nine point nine,

00:15:28.272 --> 00:15:31.510
<v The Heroine>for this week's anthem — do not skip it.

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:35.090
<v The Heroine>It's this week's lesson, in platform
sandals.

00:15:42.070 --> 00:15:44.950
<v The Heroine>Don't just learn from books; learn from
hooks.

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:51.730
<v The Heroine>And if this is your first Wednesday with
us, stop by Makeover on Main for your
residence card.

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:56.550
<v The Heroine>Two minutes, free, and everything you
collect starts counting.

00:15:57.350 --> 00:16:04.540
<v The Heroine>So remember, LAiDIES: AI can't read your
mind — so tell it what you want... what

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:06.670
<v The Heroine>you really, really want.

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:10.110
<v The Heroine>See you next Wednesday... in SUNNYVAiLE.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:16.819
